    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122422",
      "knowledge_point_id": "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(5,2)$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨5,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨2\\sqrt{3+1}+{C}_{1},\\,-3+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨5,2⟩ & =⟨4+{C}_{1},-3+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t+1}+1,\\,5-t⟩.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
