    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351145",
      "knowledge_point_id": "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,6)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the components of the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$\n\n$r(t)=⟨\\,-{e}^{-4t}+2\\,,$\n{{field-1}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{e}^{-4t},\\,5{e}^{5t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 5{e}^{5t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{e}^{-4t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,6⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-{e}^{-4(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{5(0)}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,6⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2$ and ${C}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-{e}^{-4t}+2,\\,{e}^{5t}+5⟩.\n$$",
