      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$",
