    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351151",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,2⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=e.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(e)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{2t},\\,\\frac{1}{3t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{1}{3t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(1)=⟨1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=e,$ we substitute $t=e$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(e) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}(1)+1,\\frac{1}{3}(1)+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
