      "math_academy_id": "q-351156",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨-1,\\,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r(1)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6{t}^{2},\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\sqrt{(0)^{3}}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-1$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2{t}^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{3}-1,\\,\\sqrt{(1)^{3}}+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-1,\\,1+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
