    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-83454",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})-2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
