    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-85611",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,2⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2{e}^{2t},\\,3{t}^{2}+2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (3{t}^{2}+2t)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2t}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{e}^{2(0)}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨{e}^{2(1)},\\,(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{2},\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
