    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-351223",
      "knowledge_point_id": "49aa6b33-42fb-5f28-89c5-019eadcaf1a0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Times When a Particle Is at a Certain Location Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨1,\\,-2⟩$ when $t=0,$ find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The particle intersects the $x$-axis when its position is $r=⟨x,0⟩,$ where $x$ is arbitrary. So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨8(1+2t)^{3},\\,4t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 8(1+2t)^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(1+2t)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨1,-2⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(1+0)^{4}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+2t)^{4},\\,2{t}^{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times when the particle intersects the $x$-axis, we solve for the times at which the $y$-coordinate of the position vector is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-1)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =1,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t$ must be positive, we conclude that the particle intersects the $x$-axis when $t=1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
