      "math_academy_id": "q-85616",
      "knowledge_point_id": "49aa6b33-42fb-5f28-89c5-019eadcaf1a0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Times When a Particle Is at a Certain Location Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨4t,3{t}^{2}-1⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-2,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4t,3{t}^{2}-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (3{t}^{2}-1)\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-2,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{0}^{3}-0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-2$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨0,0⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2}-2,\\,{t}^{3}-t⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-2=2(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ {t}^{3}-t=t(t-1)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions that satisfy both equations are $t=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, we conclude that the particle is at the origin  when $t=\\pm 1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "t=\\pm 1"
            },
