    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-7218",
      "knowledge_point_id": "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na=⟨0,5⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(3,-3)$ and has velocity $⟨1,0⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨0,5⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 0\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨1,\\,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,5(2)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=1$ and ${C}_{2}=-10.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1,\\,5t-10⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (5t-10)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(2)=⟨3,-3⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-10(2)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-3⟩ & =⟨2+{C}_{3},\\,-10+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=1$ and ${C}_{4}=7.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨t+1,\\,\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}-10t+7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
