  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-625",
      "knowledge_point_id": "47989094-35d2-561f-add4-f1d427cd8a20",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 3t^2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(1,2)$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 1,2 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)^{3}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,2⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=1$ and $C_2=2.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}+1,\\,{t}^{3}+2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-626",
      "knowledge_point_id": "5ff12d64-7e03-596b-83e6-c91d22600452",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Position Vector Given a Velocity Vector and Time",
      "problem": "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-627",
      "knowledge_point_id": "49aa6b33-42fb-5f28-89c5-019eadcaf1a0",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Times When a Particle Is at a Certain Location Given Its Velocity Vector",
      "problem": "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle 2t, 1 \\rangle\\,,\\quad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle -1,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is at the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "The particle is at the origin when its position is $\\mathbf{r} = \\left< 0,0 \\right>.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle -1, \\, 1 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,\\,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve for the times at which the position is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-1,\\,t+1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 1, \\\\ t+1 & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t= -1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is at the origin.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-7343",
      "knowledge_point_id": "279c5e4e-db19-584b-becb-10864fcba188",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Position Vector Given an Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}(t) =  \\left<   9\\cos {3t},\\, \\sin{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ and has velocity $\\left\\langle 2,\\,0  \\right\\rangle$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{v} (0)= \\langle 2,\\, 0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,\\,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},-1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=2$ and $C_2=1.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\left<      3\\sin{3t} +2 , \\,    1-\\cos{t} \\right>.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+2\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+2t+{C}_{3},\\,t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r} (0)= \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)+{C}_{3},\\,0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{3},\\,{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_3=1$ and $C_4=0.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left<  -\\cos {3t} + 2t + 1 , \\,   t-\\sin{t}  \\right>.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
